course: politics
lesson: 6
title: Congress and Parliament: how a law is made
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A law starts as a bill, a plan for a law, and it must pass a series of tests before everyone has to obey it: it is read out, argued over, checked line by line by a small group called a committee, changed, and voted on.
explain: Picture your class making a rule about phones at lunch: first someone reads it out, then everyone argues, then a few children fix the wording, then the class votes; Parliament and Congress do the same, only slower and more carefully.
explain: Both have two parts: the US Congress has the House of Representatives and the Senate, and the UK Parliament has the elected House of Commons, with its green benches and loud "Hear, hear!", and the House of Lords, with red benches and members who are chosen, not voted in.
explain: A bill can bounce between the two parts like a ping-pong ball until they agree; in the USA the president can then sign it or veto it, and Congress can still override a veto if two-thirds of both parts vote again.
explain: Every MP or member of Congress represents an area, and their job includes listening to everyone there, even a 9-year-old who writes about a dangerous crossing, or the many people who sign an online petition.
fact: A bill is a plan for a new law that has not been agreed yet.
fact: The US Congress makes the laws and has two parts, the House of Representatives and the Senate; a bill must win a vote in both, and then the president signs it.
fact: In the House of Commons, MPs (Members of Parliament), each voted in by one area, debate face to face, with the government on one side and the opposition on the other; the Speaker keeps order and calls "Order!"
fact: Members of the House of Lords, called peers, are appointed, not elected, often as experts or long-serving politicians; they can send a bill back with changes but cannot block the Commons forever.
fact: A bill goes through readings (debates), a committee that checks it line by line, and amendments (changes) in both parts before it becomes law.
fact: A veto is when a US president refuses to sign a bill; Congress can override it if two-thirds of both the House and the Senate vote for it again.
fact: A constituent is a person who lives in the area an MP represents; anyone can write to their MP or member of Congress, including children.
fact: Every US state has two senators, big or small, but in the House, states with more people get more members: California has more than fifty, Wyoming has one.
fact: When the Commons and the Lords disagree, a bill goes back and forth until they agree; this is called parliamentary ping-pong, and the elected Commons usually gets its way in the end.
fact: Whips try to make their party's MPs vote together; an MP who votes against their own party is called a rebel, and it happens in Congress too.
fact: In the UK, anyone can start a petition on the Parliament website: at 10,000 signatures the government replies, and at 100,000 it is considered for a debate.
fact: A debate on a petition does not mean the law changes; it puts the idea in front of MPs.
fact: Some people say the two-part system stops rushed or silly laws; others say it makes things too slow because one part can block what most people want.
exercise: Jake hears a bill "passed the House but still has to get through the Senate". Why vote twice? | In the US a bill must pass both parts of Congress to be checked twice | The first vote was cancelled because of rain | The Senate counts the House's votes again | Only the second vote really counts; the first one is just for practice
exercise: What is a bill? | A plan for a new law that has not been agreed yet | A piece of paper that says how much tax you owe this year | A law that has already been in place for many years | The name of the person who is in charge of running Parliament
exercise: Mia sees MPs on green benches shouting "Hear, hear!" Where is that? | The House of Commons in London | The House of Lords in London | The US Senate in Washington | The Supreme Court in Washington
exercise: Who keeps order in the House of Commons and calls "Order!"? | The Speaker | The King | The whip | The prime minister
exercise: Oisin spots people in red robes on red benches. "Who voted for them?" Why does Gran say "Nobody"? | Members of the House of Lords are appointed, not elected | They won an election held only for people who wear red robes | They are actors paid to sit there on TV | They were elected by the King's family
exercise: What can the House of Lords do to a bill? | Send it back with changes, though it cannot block the Commons forever | Throw it away for good with one vote | Turn it into a law without the Commons | Nothing at all; it only watches
exercise: Priya's class makes a phone rule "like Parliament". What is the right order? | Read it out, argue, fix the wording, then vote | Vote first, then decide afterwards what the rule says | Let the teacher write it down alone with no talking at all | Ask the King to choose a rule for the class and post it
exercise: Noah sees "President vetoes bill". What does that mean? | The president refused to sign it | The president signed it straight away | The president wrote the bill himself | The president sent it to the King
exercise: Can a US president say no to every bill forever? | No, Congress can override a veto with two-thirds of both parts | Yes, once he says no, a veto can never be beaten by anyone at all | No, because presidents are not allowed to veto | Yes, but only if the Supreme Court agrees
exercise: Grace writes to her MP about a crossing with no lollipop lady. Why does she get a reply? | Her MP represents everyone in her area, even children | MPs only reply to people who have given them money for their party | The letter was sent to the King by mistake | Children are allowed to vote for their MP
exercise: Tyler's Wyoming and his cousin's California both have two senators. Why? | Every state gets two senators, whatever its size | Both states have exactly the same number of people | Senators are chosen by lottery each year | Wyoming borrowed one of California's senators
exercise: In the House of Representatives, why does California have far more members than Wyoming? | States with more people get more members in the House | California simply pays more money for each of its seats | Wyoming has chosen to send fewer members to save money | The president decides each state's number of members every year
exercise: Amira's dad calls the Lords and Commons sending a bill back and forth "parliamentary ping-pong". What is going on? | The two parts disagree on changes and pass the bill back and forth | MPs and peers are playing a real table-tennis match in the building | The bill has been lost somewhere in the building | Both parts are refusing to read the bill at all
exercise: Some MPs voted against their own party. What are they called? | Rebels | Whips | Peers | Speakers
exercise: Dylan's mum says, "The whips will be cross." Who are the whips? | Party members whose job is to keep their MPs voting together | People who look after the horses and carriages kept at Parliament | The judges who decide if a law is fair | The voters who live in an MP's area
exercise: Ruby's brother's petition reaches 100,000 signatures. What happens? | Parliament considers debating it, but the law may not change | The law changes automatically the very next morning, as promised | The prime minister must resign that day | Nothing, because petitions are never read
exercise: What happens when a UK petition reaches 10,000 signatures? | The government sends a reply | Parliament must hold a debate | The law is changed on the spot | The King must sign it at once
test: Your mate says, "Parliament's well mardy, just a bunch of grown-ups yelling." ("Mardy" means moody and sulky.) What is the best reply? | It's noisy, but the arguing tests ideas in public before laws are made | He's right, they just shout all day because they have nothing else to do | The shouting is how the votes are counted | Only the King is allowed to speak there
test: Your cousin goes, "The Lords are bussin', they just bin any law they don't like." ("Bussin'" means great.) What should be put right? | The Lords can send a bill back with changes, but cannot block the Commons forever | He's right, the Lords can throw out any law forever | The Lords make all the laws without the Commons | The Lords have no part in making laws at all
test: What would happen if a bill skipped the committee stage and went straight to a final vote? | Mistakes in the wording might not be spotted before everyone had to obey it | The law would definitely turn out better and fairer, because it would be quicker | The bill would turn into a petition | Nothing; committees never change anything
test: A kid from Wyoming boasts, "We got two senators just like California, so we're just as big, y'all." What's the truth? | Every state gets two senators whatever its size; the House gives more seats for more people | Wyoming has exactly as many people as California | Senators are given out by how big a state is on the map | Wyoming has more House members than California
test: Your brother says, "Writing to an MP when you're nine? That's cap, they bin it." ("Cap" means a lie.) Who is right? | Grace, because MPs represent everyone in their area, including children | Her brother, because MPs only ever read the letters that come from voters | Her brother, because children cannot send letters | Neither, because MPs never get any letters
test: A Cork aunt says, "That petition hit a hundred thousand, the craic is mighty, it'll be law by Friday!" ("Craic" means fun and chat.) What is true? | It will be considered for a debate in the UK Parliament, which does not mean it becomes law | It becomes law on Friday, as she says | Petitions are only for people living in Ireland | Petitions with that many names are thrown away
test: A cartoon shows a president writing a law alone and it becoming law at once. What should be fixed? | In the US, Congress passes bills in both parts, and the president signs or vetoes them | Nothing, presidents write all laws alone | The King signs all US laws | The Supreme Court writes all the laws itself and then gives them to the president to sign
test: An MP votes against her party because she thinks the plan is bad for her area. Her party says she let the team down. What is the fairest summary? | Some say MPs should follow the party's promises, others say they should follow their conscience and area | She broke the law and must go to prison | MPs must always vote the way the whips say | Rebels lose their right to vote forever
test: Dad, in plain dry words, says, "Veto overridden: two-thirds in both chambers." What happened? | Congress passed the law anyway after the president said no | The president changed his mind after all and signed it himself | The bill was thrown out for good and can never be brought back | Both chambers lost two-thirds of their members in an election
test: A Northern lad says, "Ey up, our lass wrote to t'MP 'cause there's no lollipop lady at t'ginnel by school." ("Ginnel" means a narrow alley; "our lass" means his sister.) What is she doing? | Asking her representative to help fix a local safety problem | Asking the King to lower the voting age so that children can vote | Starting a new political party | Asking for a recount in the election
test: What would happen if the Lords and the Commons never agreed and nothing could end the ping-pong? | The bill could be stuck forever, which is why the elected Commons usually gets its way in the end | The bill would become law twice | The Lords would become the Commons | The bill would be sent to the US Congress
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the legislative procedure of the United States? | A bill must be approved by both the House of Representatives and the Senate prior to presentation to the President | A bill becomes law upon its approval by the House of Representatives alone | A bill becomes law upon its signature by the President irrespective of Congress | A bill is enacted solely by decision of the Supreme Court
exam: Ascertain the proportion of each chamber of Congress whose assent is requisite for the overriding of a presidential veto. | Two-thirds | One-half | Three-quarters | One-third
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the House of Lords. | Its members are appointed rather than elected, and it may amend and delay but not indefinitely obstruct legislation | Its members are elected by the citizens every four years | It possesses unlimited power to reject any bill in perpetuity | It has no function whatsoever in the making of law
exam: It is given that a petition on the Parliament website has attracted one hundred thousand signatures. Identify the consequence. | The petition shall be considered for debate in Parliament | The petition shall be enacted as law forthwith | The Prime Minister shall be required to resign | The petition shall be referred to the monarch for signature
